# Proud history, part 3: Brittany and the saints, the revival of the language, more famous people.
# Same voice as pride.py. data.py, long.py and features.py pull these in at their ends.

STORIES = [
 dict(id="brittany", era="saints", art="ship", mins=5,
  en="Wales and Brittany: one people, two shores", cy="Cymru a Llydaw: un bobl, dwy lan",
  sumEn="Macsen Wledig led the men of Britain across the sea, the saints of Wales followed, and a second Britain grew up in Gaul that still speaks our sister tongue.",
  sumCy="Arweiniodd Macsen Wledig wŷr Prydain dros y môr, dilynodd saint Cymru, a thyfodd ail Brydain yng Ngâl sy’n dal i siarad ein chwaer iaith.",
  pEn=["Across the sea from Cornwall lies a country whose name means ‘Little Britain’. Its people call it Breizh, the French call it Bretagne, and in Welsh it is Llydaw. It was founded by Britons, its saints were trained in Wales, and its language is the nearest living relative that Welsh has.",
   "## Macsen Wledig",
   "In 383 the army in Britain raised its commander, Magnus Maximus, to be emperor of Rome. He crossed to Gaul with the fighting men of Britain behind him and ruled the West for five years. Wales has never forgotten him. As Macsen Wledig he stands at the head of the pedigrees of its kings; the Pillar of Eliseg near Llangollen names him as forefather of the house of Powys; and the tale Breuddwyd Macsen tells how he dreamed of a maiden in a castle at the mouth of the Seiont, found her at Caernarfon, and made Elen of the Hosts his empress. The Roman roads of Wales are still called Sarn Helen after her.",
   "Welsh tradition holds that the soldiers who followed Macsen did not come home. Under Cynan Meiriadog, Elen’s brother, they were given land in Armorica, the north-west corner of Gaul, and settled there. That, the old histories say, was the beginning of Brittany.",
   "## The saints cross over",
   "In the two centuries that followed, more Britons crossed the Channel, and the saints went with them. Nearly all the founders of the Breton church were Welshmen or were trained in Wales. Samson, a pupil of Illtud at Llanilltud Fawr and abbot of Ynys Bŷr, Caldey, founded the bishopric of Dol. Paul Aurelian, from the same school, founded Saint-Pol-de-Léon. Malo, trained at Llancarfan, gave his name to Saint-Malo. Brioc of Ceredigion founded Saint-Brieuc, Tudwal founded Tréguier, and Padarn of Llanbadarn is honoured at Vannes. Gildas, the first historian of the Britons, ended his days at Rhuys. When the yellow plague struck Wales, Teilo took his people to Samson at Dol, and came home when it had passed.",
   "They are remembered as the founding saints of Brittany, and to this day Bretons walk the Tro Breizh, the pilgrimage round their seven cathedrals. Hundreds of Breton parishes bear the names of Welsh saints.",
   "## The same words",
   "A Welsh speaker in Brittany sees home on every signpost. A llan in Wales is a lan in Brittany, a plwyf is a plou, a tref is a tre, and aber and pen are the same in both. Bara is bread and gwin is wine in each language; mor is the sea; the numbers run un, daou, tri beside un, dau, tri. Two regions of Brittany, Domnonée and Cornouaille, carry the names the settlers brought from Devon and Cornwall. Onion sellers from Roscoff, the Sioni Winwns, worked the streets of Wales for a century and found they could make themselves understood.",
   "## A debt repaid",
   "In 1471 a fourteen-year-old Welsh boy, Henry Tudor, was driven by a storm on to the Breton coast. Brittany sheltered him for fourteen years. When he came back, he landed in Pembrokeshire under the red dragon and won the crown.",
   "The bond holds. The Breton national anthem, Bro Gozh ma Zadoù, ‘Old Land of My Fathers’, is sung to the tune of Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau. Towns across Wales are twinned with towns in Brittany, and the two peoples meet each year at the great festival of Lorient. Fifteen hundred years after the saints sailed, the two shores still know each other."],
  pCy=["Dros y môr o Gernyw mae gwlad y mae ei henw’n golygu ‘Prydain Fach’. Mae ei phobl yn ei galw’n Breizh, y Ffrancwyr yn ei galw’n Bretagne, ac yn Gymraeg, Llydaw yw hi. Brythoniaid a’i sefydlodd, yng Nghymru y hyfforddwyd ei saint, a’i hiaith yw’r perthynas byw agosaf sydd gan y Gymraeg.",
   "## Macsen Wledig",
   "Yn 383 cododd y fyddin ym Mhrydain ei chadlywydd, Magnus Maximus, yn ymerawdwr Rhufain. Croesodd i Âl â rhyfelwyr Prydain y tu ôl iddo a rheoli’r Gorllewin am bum mlynedd. Nid anghofiodd Cymru ef erioed. Fel Macsen Wledig saif ar ben achau ei brenhinoedd; mae Piler Eliseg ger Llangollen yn ei enwi’n gyndad tŷ Powys; ac mae Breuddwyd Macsen yn adrodd sut y breuddwydiodd am forwyn mewn caer wrth aber afon Seiont, ei chael yng Nghaernarfon, a gwneud Elen Luyddog yn ymerodres iddo. Gelwir ffyrdd Rhufeinig Cymru’n Sarn Helen ar ei hôl hyd heddiw.",
   "Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, ni ddaeth y milwyr a ddilynodd Macsen adref. Dan Gynan Meiriadog, brawd Elen, cawsant dir yn Armorica, cornel ogledd-orllewinol Gâl, ac ymsefydlu yno. Dyna, medd yr hen hanesion, ddechrau Llydaw.",
   "## Y saint yn croesi",
   "Yn y ddwy ganrif wedyn croesodd rhagor o Frythoniaid y Sianel, ac aeth y saint gyda hwy. Cymry, neu rai a hyfforddwyd yng Nghymru, oedd bron pob un o sylfaenwyr eglwys Llydaw. Sefydlodd Samson, disgybl i Illtud yn Llanilltud Fawr ac abad Ynys Bŷr, esgobaeth Dol. Sefydlodd Pawl Aurelian, o’r un ysgol, Saint-Pol-de-Léon. Rhoddodd Malo, a hyfforddwyd yn Llancarfan, ei enw i Saint-Malo. Sefydlodd Brioc o Geredigion Saint-Brieuc, sefydlodd Tudwal Tréguier, ac anrhydeddir Padarn o Lanbadarn yn Vannes. Yn Rhuys y diweddodd Gildas, hanesydd cyntaf y Brythoniaid, ei ddyddiau. Pan drawodd y fad felen Gymru, aeth Teilo â’i bobl at Samson yn Dol, a dod adref wedi iddi gilio.",
   "Cofir hwy fel saint sylfaenol Llydaw, a hyd heddiw mae Llydawyr yn cerdded y Tro Breizh, y bererindod o gwmpas eu saith eglwys gadeiriol. Mae cannoedd o blwyfi Llydaw’n dwyn enwau saint Cymru.",
   "## Yr un geiriau",
   "Mae siaradwr Cymraeg yn Llydaw’n gweld cartref ar bob arwyddbost. Lan yw llan yn Llydaw, plou yw plwyf, tre yw tref, ac mae aber a pen yr un fath yn y ddwy. Bara yw bara a gwin yw gwin yn y ddwy iaith; mor yw’r môr; mae’r rhifau’n rhedeg un, daou, tri wrth ochr un, dau, tri. Mae dwy ardal yn Llydaw, Domnonée a Cornouaille, yn dwyn yr enwau a ddaeth yr ymsefydlwyr gyda hwy o Ddyfnaint a Chernyw. Bu gwerthwyr winwns o Roscoff, y Sioni Winwns, yn gweithio strydoedd Cymru am ganrif, a chael y gallent eu gwneud eu hunain yn ddealladwy.",
   "## Talu’r ddyled",
   "Yn 1471 gyrrwyd bachgen pedair ar ddeg oed o Gymro, Harri Tudur, gan storm ar arfordir Llydaw. Rhoddodd Llydaw loches iddo am bedair blynedd ar ddeg. Pan ddaeth yn ôl, glaniodd yn Sir Benfro dan y ddraig goch ac ennill y goron.",
   "Mae’r cwlwm yn dal. Cenir anthem genedlaethol Llydaw, Bro Gozh ma Zadoù, ‘Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau’, ar dôn Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau. Mae trefi ledled Cymru wedi’u gefeillio â threfi yn Llydaw, ac mae’r ddwy bobl yn cyfarfod bob blwyddyn yng ngŵyl fawr Lorient. Bymtheg canrif wedi i’r saint hwylio, mae’r ddwy lan yn dal i adnabod ei gilydd."]),

 dict(id="revival", era="modern", art="torch", mins=7,
  en="Gwynfor, Plaid Cymru and the channel a fast won", cy="Gwynfor, Plaid Cymru a’r sianel a enillwyd drwy ympryd",
  sumEn="A party founded by six people in a Pwllheli café, a drowned valley, a by-election that changed everything, and one man ready to starve for a television channel in Welsh.",
  sumCy="Plaid a sefydlwyd gan chwech o bobl mewn caffi ym Mhwllheli, cwm a foddwyd, isetholiad a newidiodd bopeth, ac un gŵr a oedd yn barod i lwgu dros sianel deledu Gymraeg.",
  pEn=["In 1900 half the people of Wales spoke Welsh, and the language had no place in law, in government or in most schools. A century and a quarter later it is an official language with its own parliament, its own television channel and schools in every county. That did not happen by itself. People made it happen.",
   "## A party is born",
   "On 5 August 1925, during the National Eisteddfod at Pwllheli, six people met in a café and founded Plaid Genedlaethol Cymru, the National Party of Wales. Among them were Saunders Lewis, the Reverend Lewis Valentine and H. R. Jones. Their first aim was simple: a Wales that lived its life in Welsh.",
   "In 1936 the government built an RAF bombing school at Penyberth in Llŷn, on the site of an old Welsh house, against the protests of half a million Welsh people. Saunders Lewis, Lewis Valentine and D. J. Williams set fire to the builders’ huts and then walked to the police station and gave themselves up. A Welsh jury at Caernarfon would not convict them, so the trial was moved to London. They served nine months, and came home to a crowd of fifteen thousand. It is remembered as Tân yn Llŷn, the Fire in Llŷn.",
   "## Gwynfor",
   "Gwynfor Evans of Barry learned Welsh as a young man and gave his life to Wales. He led Plaid Cymru for thirty-six years, from 1945 to 1981, with courtesy and a will of iron. He was a pacifist, a market gardener in Llangadog and a county councillor before he was anything in Westminster.",
   "In 1957 Parliament voted to drown the village of Capel Celyn to make a reservoir for Liverpool. Not one Welsh MP voted for it. Gwynfor led the fight, and when the valley was flooded in 1965, Wales understood how little its voice counted. ‘Cofiwch Dryweryn’, Remember Tryweryn, has been painted on Welsh walls ever since.",
   "On 14 July 1966 the answer came. At the Carmarthen by-election Gwynfor won Plaid Cymru’s first seat in Parliament, and the crowd in Guildhall Square sang until the small hours. Welsh politics was never the same again. Within a year there was a Welsh Language Act; within thirteen there was a referendum on an Assembly.",
   "## The fate of the language",
   "In February 1962 Saunders Lewis gave a radio lecture, Tynged yr Iaith, the Fate of the Language. Welsh, he said, would be dead by the new century unless people were ready to make it impossible to govern Wales without it. That summer Cymdeithas yr Iaith Gymraeg, the Welsh Language Society, was founded. Its young members sat down on Trefechan Bridge in Aberystwyth, painted out English-only road signs, refused to pay bills that came in English alone, and went to prison by the hundred. They won bilingual road signs, bilingual forms and a place for Welsh in the courts.",
   "Parents did their part. The first Welsh-medium school was opened in Aberystwyth in 1939 by Ifan ab Owen Edwards, founder of the Urdd. Llanelli opened the first run by a council in 1947, and Rhydfelen, the first Welsh-medium secondary school in the south, followed in 1962. There are now hundreds.",
   "## The fast",
   "By the 1970s television was in every home and almost all of it was in English. Both main parties promised a Welsh-language channel at the election of 1979. In September that year the new government announced that it would not keep its word.",
   "Two thousand people pledged to refuse their television licences. And on 5 May 1980 Gwynfor Evans, then sixty-seven, announced that from 6 October he would fast until the promise was kept, or until he died. Nobody who knew him doubted that he meant it. Through that summer he addressed meetings of thousands the length of Wales. On 17 September the government gave way.",
   "Sianel Pedwar Cymru, S4C, went on the air on 1 November 1982. It exists because one man was ready to give his life for it, and a nation stood behind him.",
   "## Yma o hyd",
   "In 1983 Dafydd Iwan wrote a song for that moment: Yma o Hyd, ‘Still Here’. It begins with Macsen Wledig leaving Wales in 383 and ends: ‘er gwaetha pawb a phopeth, ry’n ni yma o hyd’, in spite of everyone and everything, we are still here. In 2022 the Wales football team sang it on the pitch on their way to the World Cup, and the whole stadium sang with them.",
   "Welsh has had official status since 2011. A quarter of the children of Wales are taught through it, and the aim is a million speakers by 2050. The language that was shut out of the courts in 1536 and beaten out of children with the Welsh Not is spoken in the Senedd every day."],
  pCy=["Yn 1900 siaradai hanner pobl Cymru Gymraeg, ac nid oedd i’r iaith le yn y gyfraith, mewn llywodraeth nac yn y rhan fwyaf o ysgolion. Ganrif a chwarter yn ddiweddarach mae’n iaith swyddogol â’i senedd ei hun, ei sianel deledu ei hun ac ysgolion ym mhob sir. Ni ddigwyddodd hynny ohono’i hun. Pobl a’i gwnaeth.",
   "## Geni plaid",
   "Ar 5 Awst 1925, yn ystod yr Eisteddfod Genedlaethol ym Mhwllheli, cyfarfu chwech o bobl mewn caffi a sefydlu Plaid Genedlaethol Cymru. Yn eu plith roedd Saunders Lewis, y Parchedig Lewis Valentine a H. R. Jones. Syml oedd eu nod cyntaf: Cymru a fyddai’n byw ei bywyd yn Gymraeg.",
   "Yn 1936 cododd y llywodraeth ysgol fomio i’r Awyrlu ym Mhenyberth yn Llŷn, ar safle hen dŷ Cymreig, er gwaethaf protest hanner miliwn o Gymry. Rhoddodd Saunders Lewis, Lewis Valentine a D. J. Williams gytiau’r adeiladwyr ar dân, ac yna cerdded i swyddfa’r heddlu a’u rhoi eu hunain i fyny. Ni fynnai rheithgor Cymreig yng Nghaernarfon eu cael yn euog, felly symudwyd yr achos i Lundain. Buont yn y carchar am naw mis, a daethant adref at dorf o bymtheng mil. Fe’i cofir fel y Tân yn Llŷn.",
   "## Gwynfor",
   "Dysgodd Gwynfor Evans o’r Barri Gymraeg yn ŵr ifanc a rhoi ei oes i Gymru. Arweiniodd Blaid Cymru am un mlynedd ar bymtheg ar hugain, o 1945 hyd 1981, â chwrteisi ac ewyllys o haearn. Heddychwr ydoedd, garddwr masnachol yn Llangadog a chynghorydd sir cyn bod yn ddim yn San Steffan.",
   "Yn 1957 pleidleisiodd y Senedd dros foddi pentref Capel Celyn i wneud cronfa ddŵr i Lerpwl. Ni phleidleisiodd yr un Aelod Seneddol o Gymru drosto. Gwynfor a arweiniodd y frwydr, a phan foddwyd y cwm yn 1965, deallodd Cymru cyn lleied oedd gwerth ei llais. Mae ‘Cofiwch Dryweryn’ wedi’i beintio ar waliau Cymru byth er hynny.",
   "Ar 14 Gorffennaf 1966 daeth yr ateb. Yn isetholiad Caerfyrddin enillodd Gwynfor sedd gyntaf Plaid Cymru yn y Senedd, a chanodd y dorf ar Sgwâr y Guildhall hyd oriau mân y bore. Ni fu gwleidyddiaeth Cymru yr un fath wedyn. O fewn blwyddyn roedd Deddf yr Iaith Gymraeg; o fewn tair blynedd ar ddeg roedd refferendwm ar Gynulliad.",
   "## Tynged yr iaith",
   "Ym mis Chwefror 1962 traddododd Saunders Lewis ddarlith radio, Tynged yr Iaith. Byddai’r Gymraeg, meddai, wedi marw erbyn y ganrif newydd oni bai fod pobl yn barod i’w gwneud yn amhosibl llywodraethu Cymru hebddi. Yr haf hwnnw sefydlwyd Cymdeithas yr Iaith Gymraeg. Eisteddodd ei haelodau ifainc ar Bont Trefechan yn Aberystwyth, peintio dros arwyddion ffyrdd uniaith Saesneg, gwrthod talu biliau a ddeuai yn Saesneg yn unig, a mynd i’r carchar wrth y cannoedd. Enillasant arwyddion ffyrdd dwyieithog, ffurflenni dwyieithog a lle i’r Gymraeg yn y llysoedd.",
   "Gwnaeth rhieni eu rhan. Agorwyd yr ysgol Gymraeg gyntaf yn Aberystwyth yn 1939 gan Ifan ab Owen Edwards, sylfaenydd yr Urdd. Agorodd Llanelli’r gyntaf dan ofal cyngor yn 1947, a dilynodd Rhydfelen, yr ysgol uwchradd Gymraeg gyntaf yn y de, yn 1962. Mae cannoedd ohonynt bellach.",
   "## Yr ympryd",
   "Erbyn y 1970au roedd teledu ym mhob cartref a bron y cyfan ohono’n Saesneg. Addawodd y ddwy brif blaid sianel Gymraeg yn etholiad 1979. Ym mis Medi’r flwyddyn honno cyhoeddodd y llywodraeth newydd na fyddai’n cadw ei gair.",
   "Addawodd dwy fil o bobl wrthod talu am eu trwyddedau teledu. Ac ar 5 Mai 1980 cyhoeddodd Gwynfor Evans, ac yntau’n chwe deg saith oed, y byddai o 6 Hydref ymlaen yn ymprydio nes cedwid yr addewid, neu nes iddo farw. Nid amheuai neb a’i hadwaenai ei fod o ddifrif. Drwy’r haf hwnnw anerchodd gyfarfodydd o filoedd ar hyd a lled Cymru. Ar 17 Medi ildiodd y llywodraeth.",
   "Aeth Sianel Pedwar Cymru, S4C, ar yr awyr ar 1 Tachwedd 1982. Mae’n bod am fod un gŵr yn barod i roi ei fywyd drosti, a chenedl yn sefyll y tu ôl iddo.",
   "## Yma o hyd",
   "Yn 1983 ysgrifennodd Dafydd Iwan gân i’r foment honno: Yma o Hyd. Mae’n dechrau gyda Macsen Wledig yn gadael Cymru yn 383 ac yn gorffen: ‘er gwaetha pawb a phopeth, ry’n ni yma o hyd’. Yn 2022 canodd tîm pêl-droed Cymru hi ar y cae ar eu ffordd i Gwpan y Byd, a chanodd y stadiwm cyfan gyda hwy.",
   "Mae gan y Gymraeg statws swyddogol er 2011. Addysgir chwarter plant Cymru drwyddi, a’r nod yw miliwn o siaradwyr erbyn 2050. Mae’r iaith a gaewyd allan o’r llysoedd yn 1536 ac a gurwyd o blant â’r Welsh Not yn cael ei siarad yn y Senedd bob dydd."]),
]

