PEOPLE_LONG = {
 "cadog": (
  ["Cadog was the son of Gwynllyw, king of Gwynllŵg, and Gwladus, daughter of Brychan of Brycheiniog. He gave up a throne for learning and about the year 518 founded the monastery and school of Llancarfan in the Vale of Glamorgan.",
   "Llancarfan became one of the great schools of the age. The Irish came there to study; Finnian of Clonard, who taught the saints of Ireland, was among them. Cadog’s name is on churches across south Wales, in Cornwall, in Scotland and in Brittany, and the wise sayings of Cadog Ddoeth were treasured by the Welsh for a thousand years."],
  ["Mab oedd Cadog i Wynllyw, brenin Gwynllŵg, a Gwladus, merch Brychan Brycheiniog. Ildiodd orsedd er mwyn dysg, a thua’r flwyddyn 518 sefydlodd fynachlog ac ysgol Llancarfan ym Mro Morgannwg.",
   "Daeth Llancarfan yn un o ysgolion mawr yr oes. Deuai’r Gwyddelod yno i astudio; yn eu plith roedd Finnian o Clonard, athro saint Iwerddon. Mae enw Cadog ar eglwysi ar draws y de, yng Nghernyw, yn yr Alban ac yn Llydaw, a thrysorwyd doethinebau Cadog Ddoeth gan y Cymry am fil o flynyddoedd."]),
 "owain-lawgoch": (
  ["Owain ap Thomas ap Rhodri was the great-nephew of Llywelyn ap Gruffudd and the last heir of the house of Gwynedd in the male line. He made his name in France, where they called him Yvain de Galles and counted him among the finest captains of the age. He led a company of Welshmen in the service of the French king.",
   "In 1372 he proclaimed his right to Wales, ‘which is and should be mine by right of succession’, and sailed from Harfleur with a fleet to win it. He took Guernsey on the way before the king of France called him back to the war. The English crown feared him so much that it paid an agent to kill him, and he died at the siege of Mortagne in 1378.",
   "Wales did not forget him. The poets sang of his return, and it is told that Owain Lawgoch sleeps with his men in a cave until the day he is needed. A memorial to him was raised at Mortagne-sur-Gironde in 2003."],
  ["Gor-nai i Lywelyn ap Gruffudd oedd Owain ap Thomas ap Rhodri, ac etifedd olaf tŷ Gwynedd yn y llinach wrywaidd. Gwnaeth ei enw yn Ffrainc, lle gelwid ef yn Yvain de Galles a’i gyfrif ymhlith capteiniaid gorau’r oes. Arweiniodd gwmni o Gymry yng ngwasanaeth brenin Ffrainc.",
   "Yn 1372 cyhoeddodd ei hawl ar Gymru, ‘sydd ac a ddylai fod yn eiddo i mi drwy hawl olyniaeth’, a hwylio o Harfleur â llynges i’w hennill. Cipiodd Ynys y Garn ar y ffordd cyn i frenin Ffrainc ei alw’n ôl i’r rhyfel. Cymaint yr ofnai coron Lloegr ef nes talu i asiant ei ladd, a bu farw yng ngwarchae Mortagne yn 1378.",
   "Nid anghofiodd Cymru ef. Canodd y beirdd am ei ddychweliad, a dywedir bod Owain Lawgoch yn cysgu gyda’i wŷr mewn ogof hyd y dydd y bydd ei angen. Codwyd cofeb iddo ym Mortagne-sur-Gironde yn 2003."]),
 "robert-recorde": (
  ["Robert Recorde was born in Tenby about 1512, studied at Oxford and Cambridge, and became a physician to the royal court and controller of the mint. He wrote the first books in English on arithmetic, geometry, astronomy and algebra, in plain words that a working person could follow.",
   "In The Whetstone of Witte, in 1557, he grew tired of writing ‘is equal to’ again and again and drew two parallel lines instead, ‘because no two things can be more equal’. The equals sign has been used in every country on earth ever since. A memorial to him stands in St Mary’s Church, Tenby."],
  ["Ganed Robert Recorde yn Ninbych-y-pysgod tua 1512, astudiodd yn Rhydychen a Chaergrawnt, a daeth yn feddyg i’r llys brenhinol ac yn rheolwr y bathdy. Ysgrifennodd y llyfrau cyntaf yn Saesneg ar rifyddeg, geometreg, seryddiaeth ac algebra, mewn geiriau plaen y gallai gweithiwr eu dilyn.",
   "Yn The Whetstone of Witte, yn 1557, blinodd ar ysgrifennu ‘yn hafal i’ dro ar ôl tro a thynnu dwy linell gyfochrog yn ei le, ‘am na all dau beth fod yn fwy cyfartal’. Defnyddiwyd yr hafalnod ym mhob gwlad ar y ddaear byth er hynny. Saif cofeb iddo yn Eglwys y Santes Fair, Dinbych-y-pysgod."]),
 "william-jones": (
  ["William Jones was born in 1675 on a small farm at Llanfihangel Tre’r Beirdd on Anglesey. His gift for numbers was noticed at the village school, and he went to sea as a teacher of mathematics on a man-of-war before settling in London.",
   "In 1706, in his book Synopsis Palmariorum Matheseos, he became the first to use the Greek letter π for the ratio of a circle’s circumference to its diameter. He was a friend of Isaac Newton and Edmond Halley, a Fellow and Vice-President of the Royal Society, and the editor of some of Newton’s papers."],
  ["Ganed William Jones yn 1675 ar fferm fechan yn Llanfihangel Tre’r Beirdd ym Môn. Sylwyd ar ei ddawn â rhifau yn ysgol y pentref, ac aeth i’r môr yn athro mathemateg ar long ryfel cyn ymsefydlu yn Llundain.",
   "Yn 1706, yn ei lyfr Synopsis Palmariorum Matheseos, ef oedd y cyntaf i ddefnyddio’r llythyren Roeg π am gymhareb cylchedd cylch i’w ddiamedr. Roedd yn gyfaill i Isaac Newton ac Edmond Halley, yn Gymrawd ac yn Is-lywydd y Gymdeithas Frenhinol, ac yn olygydd rhai o bapurau Newton."]),
 "william-grove": (
  ["William Robert Grove was born in Swansea in 1811 and trained as a barrister, but his heart was in the laboratory. In 1842 he built his ‘gas voltaic battery’, which combined hydrogen and oxygen to make electricity and water. It was the first fuel cell.",
   "He also made one of the first electric lights and set out the principle of the conservation of energy a year before it was announced elsewhere. He helped to found the Royal Institution of South Wales in Swansea, became a judge of the High Court, and was knighted. A hundred and twenty years after his discovery, fuel cells powered the spacecraft that took people to the Moon."],
  ["Ganed William Robert Grove yn Abertawe yn 1811 a’i hyfforddi’n fargyfreithiwr, ond yn y labordy yr oedd ei galon. Yn 1842 adeiladodd ei ‘fatri foltäig nwy’, a gyfunai hydrogen ac ocsigen i wneud trydan a dŵr. Dyna’r gell danwydd gyntaf.",
   "Gwnaeth hefyd un o’r goleuadau trydan cyntaf a gosod allan egwyddor cadwraeth egni flwyddyn cyn ei chyhoeddi mewn man arall. Helpodd i sefydlu Sefydliad Brenhinol De Cymru yn Abertawe, daeth yn farnwr yn yr Uchel Lys, ac fe’i hurddwyd yn farchog. Gant ac ugain o flynyddoedd wedi ei ddarganfyddiad, celloedd tanwydd a yrrai’r llongau gofod a aeth â phobl i’r Lleuad."]),
 "alfred-russel-wallace": (
  ["Alfred Russel Wallace was born at Llanbadoc, near Usk, in 1823. As a young man he worked as a land surveyor in the hills around Neath, where he taught himself botany and learned to love wild places.",
   "He spent years exploring the Amazon and the islands of the Malay Archipelago, collecting more than a hundred thousand specimens. In 1858, during a fever, the idea of evolution by natural selection came to him whole. He wrote it down and sent it to Charles Darwin, and their papers were read together in London that summer. The line he found dividing the animals of Asia from those of Australia is still called the Wallace Line. He was also a lifelong campaigner for the rights of working people and for the land to belong to the nation."],
  ["Ganed Alfred Russel Wallace yn Llanbadog, ger Brynbuga, yn 1823. Yn ŵr ifanc gweithiodd fel tirfesurydd yn y bryniau o gwmpas Castell-nedd, lle dysgodd fotaneg iddo’i hun a dod i garu lleoedd gwyllt.",
   "Treuliodd flynyddoedd yn archwilio’r Amazon ac ynysoedd Ynysfor Malaya, gan gasglu mwy na chan mil o sbesimenau. Yn 1858, yn ystod twymyn, daeth y syniad o esblygiad drwy ddethol naturiol iddo’n gyfan. Ysgrifennodd ef a’i anfon at Charles Darwin, a darllenwyd eu papurau gyda’i gilydd yn Llundain yr haf hwnnw. Gelwir y llinell a ddarganfu, sy’n rhannu anifeiliaid Asia oddi wrth rai Awstralia, yn Llinell Wallace hyd heddiw. Bu hefyd yn ymgyrchydd gydol oes dros hawliau gweithwyr a thros i’r tir berthyn i’r genedl."]),
 "john-charles": (
  ["William John Charles was born in Cwmbwrla, Swansea, in 1931. Six feet two and equally brilliant at centre-half and centre-forward, he scored forty-two league goals for Leeds United in a single season, a club record that still stands.",
   "In 1957 he went to Juventus for a British record fee and became a hero of Italian football: three league titles, top scorer in his first season, and the name by which Turin still knows him, Il Gigante Buono, the Gentle Giant. In his whole career he was never booked and never sent off. He led Wales to the quarter-finals of the 1958 World Cup, where, with Charles injured, they lost by a single goal to Brazil, scored by a seventeen-year-old called Pelé."],
  ["Ganed William John Charles yng Nghwmbwrla, Abertawe, yn 1931. Yn chwe throedfedd a dwy fodfedd ac yr un mor ddisglair fel canolwr ac fel blaenwr canol, sgoriodd bedwar deg dau o goliau cynghrair i Leeds United mewn un tymor, record i’r clwb sy’n sefyll o hyd.",
   "Yn 1957 aeth i Juventus am y ffi uchaf erioed ym Mhrydain a dod yn arwr pêl-droed yr Eidal: tair pencampwriaeth, prif sgoriwr yn ei dymor cyntaf, a’r enw y mae Torino’n ei adnabod wrtho o hyd, Il Gigante Buono, y Cawr Addfwyn. Yn ei holl yrfa ni chafodd erioed gerdyn na’i anfon o’r cae. Arweiniodd Gymru i rownd wyth olaf Cwpan y Byd 1958, lle, a Charles wedi’i anafu, collasant o un gôl i Frasil, a sgoriwyd gan lanc dwy ar bymtheg oed o’r enw Pelé."]),
 "gareth-edwards": (
  ["Gareth Edwards was born in 1947 in Gwaun-cae-Gurwen, a miner’s son. He won his first cap for Wales at nineteen and was captain at twenty, the youngest Wales has ever had. Between 1967 and 1978 he played fifty-three times for his country without missing a single match.",
   "With Barry John, Phil Bennett, J. P. R. Williams, Gerald Davies and Mervyn Davies he made the Wales side of the 1970s the finest in the world: three Grand Slams and five Triple Crowns. His try for the Barbarians against New Zealand at Cardiff in 1973 is still called the greatest ever scored. In 2003 a poll of international players named him the greatest rugby player of all time."],
  ["Ganed Gareth Edwards yn 1947 yng Ngwaun-cae-Gurwen, yn fab i löwr. Enillodd ei gap cyntaf dros Gymru’n bedair ar bymtheg oed a bu’n gapten yn ugain oed, yr ieuengaf a gafodd Cymru erioed. Rhwng 1967 a 1978 chwaraeodd dair gwaith ar ddeg a deugain dros ei wlad heb golli’r un gêm.",
   "Gyda Barry John, Phil Bennett, J. P. R. Williams, Gerald Davies a Mervyn Davies gwnaeth dîm Cymru’r 1970au y gorau yn y byd: tair Camp Lawn a phum Coron Driphlyg. Gelwir ei gais dros y Barbariaid yn erbyn Seland Newydd yng Nghaerdydd yn 1973 y gorau a sgoriwyd erioed o hyd. Yn 2003 enwodd arolwg o chwaraewyr rhyngwladol ef yn chwaraewr rygbi gorau erioed."]),
 "tom-jones": (
  ["Thomas Jones Woodward was born in Treforest, Pontypridd, in 1940, the son of a coal miner. He sang in chapel, in school and in the working men’s clubs of the valleys before ‘It’s Not Unusual’ took him to number one in 1965.",
   "Sixty years of hits followed, among them ‘Green, Green Grass of Home’ and ‘Delilah’, which Welsh crowds made their own. He has sold more than a hundred million records, was knighted in 2006, and in 2021, at eighty, became the oldest man to have a number one album in Britain. Wherever he has sung, he has been the boy from Pontypridd."],
  ["Ganed Thomas Jones Woodward yn Nhrefforest, Pontypridd, yn 1940, yn fab i löwr. Canodd yn y capel, yn yr ysgol ac yng nghlybiau gweithwyr y cymoedd cyn i ‘It’s Not Unusual’ fynd ag ef i rif un yn 1965.",
   "Dilynodd trigain mlynedd o ganeuon llwyddiannus, yn eu plith ‘Green, Green Grass of Home’ a ‘Delilah’, a wnaeth torfeydd Cymru’n eiddo iddynt eu hunain. Mae wedi gwerthu mwy na chan miliwn o recordiau, fe’i hurddwyd yn farchog yn 2006, ac yn 2021, yn bedwar ugain oed, ef oedd y gŵr hynaf i gael albwm rhif un ym Mhrydain. Ble bynnag y canodd, y bachgen o Bontypridd fu ef."]),
 "anthony-hopkins": (
  ["Philip Anthony Hopkins was born in 1937 in Margam, Port Talbot, the son of a baker. A meeting with Richard Burton, from the next village, set him on his path. He trained in Cardiff and London and was chosen by Laurence Olivier for the National Theatre.",
   "He won the Academy Award for Best Actor in 1992 for The Silence of the Lambs and again in 2021 for The Father, when at eighty-three he became the oldest actor ever to win it. He was knighted in 1993. In 1998 he gave a million pounds to help the National Trust buy part of Yr Wyddfa for the nation."],
  ["Ganed Philip Anthony Hopkins yn 1937 ym Margam, Port Talbot, yn fab i bobydd. Cyfarfod â Richard Burton, o’r pentref nesaf, a’i gosododd ar ei lwybr. Hyfforddodd yng Nghaerdydd a Llundain ac fe’i dewiswyd gan Laurence Olivier i’r Theatr Genedlaethol.",
   "Enillodd Wobr yr Academi am yr Actor Gorau yn 1992 am The Silence of the Lambs ac eto yn 2021 am The Father, pan ddaeth, yn wyth deg tair oed, yr actor hynaf erioed i’w hennill. Fe’i hurddwyd yn farchog yn 1993. Yn 1998 rhoddodd filiwn o bunnoedd i helpu’r Ymddiriedolaeth Genedlaethol i brynu rhan o’r Wyddfa i’r genedl."]),
}
